":"Which real function is used as the model for defining the complex exponential?","options":["The real logarithm through its inverse formula","The real sine function through periodicity","The absolute value function through distances","The real exponential function through its Taylor series"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"14dc3744240727f62d5c7fcf"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_complex_exponential_function_35429462_003","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"Using the defining series, which expression gives the first four terms of $\\mathrm{e}^{\\mathrm{i}}$?","options":["$\\displaystyle 1+\\mathrm{i}+\\frac{1}{2!}+\\frac{\\mathrm{i}}{3!}$","$\\displaystyle 1-\\mathrm{i}-\\frac{1}{2!}+\\frac{\\mathrm{i}}{3!}$","$\\displaystyle 1+\\mathrm{i}-\\frac{1}{2!}-\\frac{\\mathrm{i}}{3!}$","$\\displaystyle \\mathrm{i}+\\frac{\\mathrm{i}}{2!}+\\frac{\\mathrm{i}}{3!}+\\frac{\\mathrm{i}}{4!}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"fa2f3094257d32e682ddc9ab"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_complex_exponential_function_35429462_004","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"For which complex numbers $z$ does the defining series for $\\mathrm{e}^z$ converge?","options":["Only for $|z| \\lt 1$","For all $z\\in\\mathbb{C}$","Only for real $z$","Only for purely imaginary $z$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"446fe9e6231694e111efb120"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_complex_exponential_function_35429462_005","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Which prescription extends the real exponential by its power series to complex inputs?","options":["Use the same power series with complex powers $z^n$.","Keep only powers of the real coordinate $\\operatorname{Re}z$.","Use powers of $|z|$ and discard the direction of $z$.","Replace the factorial coefficient $1/n!$ by $n!$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"786b0d093e53d22e8aa42370"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_complex_exponential_function_35429462_006","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"For $\\mathrm{e}^z$, what role do the terms $z^n$ play?","options":["They are powers of the modulus $|z|$ only","They are derivatives of $z$","They are arbitrary coefficients chosen to force convergence","They are powers of the complex number $z$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"17803d342a6170e01c1be982"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_complex_exponential_function_35429462_007","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"What is $\\mathrm{e}^{0}$?","options":["$0$","$1$","$\\mathrm{i}$","$\\displaystyle \\sum_{n = 1}^{\\infty}\\frac{1}{n!}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"98d38fc4c7e69f3edd3835b1"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_complex_exponential_function_35429462_008","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"What is the coefficient of $z^3$ in the series for $\\mathrm{e}^z$?","options":["$3!$","$\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{2!}$","$\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{3!}$","$\\displaystyle -\\frac{1}{3!}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"c190a80c3977c86e853cf192"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_complex_exponential_function_35429462_009","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What is the domain of the complex exponential defined by $\\mathrm e^z=\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty}z^n/n!$?","options":["The definition only works when $z$ is a real number","The definition only works when $|z| \\lt 1$","The definition excludes values of $z$ with nonzero imaginary part","Works for every $z\\in\\mathbb{C}$ since the series converges everywhere"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"4436f726ab769e690e272b79"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_complex_exponential_function_35429462_010","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Why does $\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty}z^n/n!$ define a finite complex value for every fixed $z\\in\\mathbb C$?","option