{e}^z$?","options":["$\\dfrac{z^n}{n!}$","$\\dfrac{n!}{z^n}$","$(-1)^n z^n$","$z+n!$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"070d61dd5b07747c94e10a22"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_complex_exponential_function_35429462_016","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"What is the coefficient of $z^2$ in the series for $\\mathrm{e}^z$?","options":["$2!$","$-\\dfrac{1}{2!}$","$0$","$\\dfrac{1}{2!}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"660ea2c32f5b94d5707d2fb2"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_complex_exponential_function_35429462_017","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"Using the first two terms, what is the linear truncation of $\\mathrm{e}^{2\\mathrm{i}}$?","options":["$2\\mathrm{i}$","$1-2\\mathrm{i}$","$1+2\\mathrm{i}$","$1+4\\mathrm{i}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"e65fd75964de5ff820d1aaec"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_complex_exponential_function_35429462_018","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"Which term comes immediately after $z$ in the series for $\\mathrm{e}^z$?","options":["$\\dfrac{z^2}{2!}$","$\\dfrac{z^3}{3!}$","$z^2$","$\\dfrac{1}{z}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"391ec48fedc52904ff1beaaa"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_complex_exponential_function_35429462_019","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Why can the same series formula extend the real exponential to complex inputs?","options":["Complex inputs restrict the defining series to the real axis.","Complex inputs remove every factorial from the coefficients.","Complex inputs force all but finitely many terms to vanish.","The series uses complex powers and converges for every input."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"464c13950828d635fe9a477a"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_complex_exponential_function_35429462_020","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"What is the expansion of $\\mathrm{e}^z$ through degree $4$?","options":["$1+z+\\dfrac{z^2}{2!}+\\dfrac{z^3}{3!}+\\dfrac{z^4}{4!}$","$1+z+2!z^2+3!z^3+4!z^4$","$z+\\dfrac{z^2}{2!}+\\dfrac{z^3}{3!}+\\dfrac{z^4}{4!}$","$1+\\dfrac{z}{2!}+\\dfrac{z^2}{3!}+\\dfrac{z^3}{4!}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"de63d09a2c1926d9142919c7"},{"id":"repair_complex_exponential_function_001","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What is the radius of convergence of the defining series for $\\mathrm e^z$?","options":["$1$.","$0$.","$|z|$.","Infinite."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"1b116d112ed913e6ac9ccc07"}]}}